\title{No switch enters a default set without an adversarial pass with official verdicts}
\taxon{Decision}
\date{2026-11-24}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{queclr}
\tag{soundness}
\tag{machine}
\meta{status}{accepted}
\meta{created}{2026-11-24}
\meta{origin}{rlvm-machine/NOTES-INT.md}
\import{base-macros}

\p{\strong{Status:} accepted. The rule was applied for the first time on the same day, to the candidate default of the machine. No single document states it in these words; the last paragraph says which document states which part.}

\p{\strong{Context.} Every optimisation of the machine that changes what is computed sits behind a [staging switch](design-switches), and a switch enters the default set on evidence. For the default set of the wave-6 integration that evidence was [the gate](design-gate): the differential against the reference engine, tier and convention equivalence, the audits, 17,775 mutants, the differential engine on 760,839 units, and the hostile suite as it stood, 329 exports under 32 switch sets. Ten of the thirteen switches of that set (\code{same}, \code{pchk}, \code{bodies}, \code{match}, \code{joint}, \code{lazycases}, \code{directrules}, \code{lazyeval}, \code{code}, \code{casetrees}) had entered it without an adversarial review of their own. The first soundness review had attacked the code objects and, lightly, \code{lazyeval}; the other seven were experiments or had no code when it ended, and it listed them as not reviewed. That review had already seen the pattern once: two switches were unsound four hours into a wave that had measured them clean on everything.}

\p{What happened next is the reason for the rule. The machine became the default engine on that evidence and was [withdrawn 54 minutes later](dec-T79V): 67 new hostile exports showed five wrong accepts against the official checker, three of them in the generic tier. A dedicated review of the ten switches then found, with 423 exports of its own, that two of them add accepts of their own that the official checker does not give (\code{pchk}, finding S3-F5, and \code{lazyeval}, finding S3-F4, whose "order-only" argument does not hold), that \code{code} declines valid units with an internal fault (S3-F7), and that the derived rules give a wrong accept with their default thresholds (S3-F6). None of this was visible to a gate whose oracle is the reference engine, or to hostile rows written before the shape was known.}

\p{\strong{Decision.} A switch enters a default set, and the default engine changes, only after an adversarial pass on the candidate:}

\ol{
\li{hostile exports written for the candidate by an agent that does not own the code, each judged by the official checker run on the export, never by one of the project's engines;}
\li{seeded faults in a private copy, one per switch, to show that the rows reach the switch;}
\li{every row entered in the hostile suite with its official verdict and its kind, the rows that caught the seeded faults in particular;}
\li{a written list of what the pass did not establish.}
}

\p{The pass comes before the gates are read, not after. What it must show depends on the policy: for the practical default, no accept of an underivable statement and a certificate for every accept against the official verdict; for anything that is to be exact, the official verdict. See [the review method](design-adversarial-review) and [the exactness contract](design-exactness-contract).}

\p{\strong{Evidence.} What a review sees and a differential gate does not, in numbers of that day: 0 differences on 760,839 units against 5 wrong accepts from 67 exports; 0 unexpected verdicts on 10,528 judgments of the old suite against 7 findings from 423 new exports. And what a seeded fault is for: of 11 faults planted in the ten switches, 3 were invisible to the reviewer's own first set of rows; of 4 planted in the candidate default, 3 were. Each pointed at a path no row had reached.}

\p{\strong{How it is applied.} The procedure for changing a default in \src{rlvm-machine/SWITCHES.md} has three steps: the owner of the switch sends the evidence; the integrator re-runs the gate and the mutants on the published tree, asks for open soundness findings and measures the candidate against the current default in one quiet window; the default constant, the list of switches and its test change together. The adversarial pass supplies the second step's answer. The end-to-end guard \src{scripts/gate.sh} runs the hostile rows through the launcher and judges each by its official verdict and kind, and with its option for default settings it runs the distribution exactly as a user does.}

\p{The first application was the second pass of the review of the default set, on the candidate default: the default set with the switch \code{eager} and the two proof-comparison shortcuts. Result: no accept of an underivable statement on 987 hostile rows, with four conditions attached. The named switches for the two shortcuts must turn on the behaviour they depend on; the new rows must enter the suite with their kinds; the declines of S3-F7 must be fixed or counted; and the strict re-check of the pipeline must stay what decides a rejection of the machine.}

\p{\strong{Consequences.}}

\ul{
\li{The derived rules need a new adversarial pass before any gate is read for them; see [the decision](dec-MOCU).}
\li{A switch can still be published as an experiment, off by default, on the gates alone. The switch \code{eager} was published that way and reviewed as part of the candidate.}
\li{The rule does not make the default exact. The pass that found no underivable accept also recorded the accepts that the official checker does not give; they are [the divergence classes](design-official-divergence).}
\li{Known gaps of the passes made so far: they ran on the JVM launcher with one worker; the typed bindings were exercised by rows and not attacked on their own; \code{same} was found exact relative to the generic tier as coded, with one skipped-comparison question looked for and not excluded.}
}

\p{Where the parts are stated: the lesson, that a review with new hostile exports belongs before a change of the default engine, in \src{rlvm-machine/NOTES-INT.md} section 3; the remark that a re-run matrix is not a review, in the addendum of \src{docs/design/review/soundness-machine.md}; the requirement of a review that found no false accept, in note 13 of \src{docs/design/OWNER-NOTES.md}; seeded faults and the conditions on the candidate, in \src{docs/design/review/soundness-default-set.md} sections 6, 7 and 10; the procedure, in \src{rlvm-machine/SWITCHES.md} under "Changing a default". Source: [the wave-6 session](ses-CVTE) and [the exactness session](ses-874D).}

\p{Findings, examples and open problems: [what a gate against the reference engine cannot see](ins-SJW4), [what a seeded fault shows](ins-TNZD).}
